# apidocgen

Scan a Java code base on disk (no git needed), keep a **persistent code graph**, and generate
Persian **Datin-style service documents** (like `ESB.IssueDocument…pdf`) for every HTTP endpoint —
while sending each piece of code to the LLM **at most once**.

```
   source folders ──► scanner ──► parser (pure Python) ──► code graph (SQLite)
                                                              │
                                    endpoint detectors ◄──────┤   Spring MVC/WebFlux, JAX-RS, Servlets,
                                                              │   Micronaut, RouterFunction, custom rules,
                          ┌────── deterministic doc model ◄───┘   manual list, LLM-assisted discovery
                          │       (fields, types, required flags, enums, samples, error codes)
                          │
                          ├────── analysis units + content hashes ──► cache hit? ──► reuse
                          │                                            miss?     ──► LLM (Persian descriptions)
                          ▼
                    HTML document (RTL, print-ready)  +  archive  +  web UI
```

* **Zero native dependencies** – only `Jinja2` and `PyYAML`. Works on macOS/Linux/Windows with Python 3.9+.
* **Frameworks**: Spring (`@RestController`, `@RequestMapping`, `@GetMapping`…, `RouterFunction` routes, API interfaces),
  JAX-RS (`@Path`, `@GET`…, Jersey/RESTEasy/Quarkus), Servlets (`@WebServlet`, `doGet/doPost`), Micronaut,
  **config-driven custom rules** for in-house dispatchers, a **manual endpoints file**, and an optional
  **LLM-assisted `discover`** step for code with no annotations at all.
* **Token economy**: DTOs and endpoints are analysed as small *units* whose cache key is the SHA-256 of the code the
  model would see. Unchanged code is never re-sent; a comment change re-sends one DTO; a new field re-sends that DTO and
  only the endpoints that exchange it. Anthropic prompt caching is used for the static instructions.
* **LLM providers**: Anthropic (default `claude-sonnet-5`), any OpenAI-compatible server (OpenAI, Azure, LM Studio,
  vLLM…), Ollama, and a deterministic `mock` for tests / dry runs.
* **Output**: one self-contained HTML per run (cover page, شناسنامه/تاییدیه/جدول تغییرات, فهرست مطالب, فهرست جداول, مقدمه,
  per-service tables, نکات, ساختار خروجی موفق/ناموفق, مراحل فراخوانی, جدول کد خطاها, پیوست) that prints to A4 with a
  repeating header/footer. Every render is archived.
* **UI**: `apidocgen ui` – applications, endpoint tree, document archive, scan/analyze/render buttons, dark mode.

---

## 1. Install

```bash
cd apidocgen
python3 -m pip install .            # or:  pip install -e .   (editable)
apidocgen --version
```

No install at all is also fine: `pip install -r requirements.txt` and use `python3 -m apidocgen …` from this folder
(add the folder to `PYTHONPATH` to run it from elsewhere).

Optional extras: `pip install Markdown` (markdown intro/appendix files), `pip install tiktoken` (exact token estimates).

## 2. Quick start

```bash
cd /path/to/my-java-service
apidocgen init                       # writes a commented apidocgen.yaml
# edit apidocgen.yaml: project.paths, llm.provider/model, doc.title/author/version ...
export ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...  # or OPENAI_API_KEY for provider: openai

apidocgen scan                       # parse changed files, rebuild the code graph, detect endpoints
apidocgen endpoints -v               # what was detected (paths, params, body/response types)
apidocgen analyze --dry-run          # what would be sent to the LLM and the estimated token cost
apidocgen analyze                    # send only the cache misses
apidocgen render                     # write docs/api-document.html (+ .json) and archive it
apidocgen status                     # graph / cache / token usage
```

`apidocgen run` does scan + analyze + render in one go (`--no-llm` renders from the cache and code comments only).
Open the HTML in Chrome/Edge and **Print → Save as PDF** for the paginated A4 document.

Try it on the bundled sample first:

```bash
cd examples/sample-esb
apidocgen run --no-llm               # uses code comments + config overrides, no LLM
apidocgen ui                         # http://127.0.0.1:8765
```

## 3. How the token saving works

| Unit kind | Content hashed | Re-sent when |
|-----------|----------------|--------------|
| `type` (one DTO / enum) | class header, fields with annotations & comments, enum constants, inherited fields | that class (or a superclass) changes |
| `endpoint` (one handler) | controller header, handler method (and its implementation when the mapping is on an interface), **skeletons** (names/types only) of the exchanged types, bodies of called project methods up to `analysis.call_depth`, referenced error-catalog constants | the handler, a called method within the depth, or the *structure* of an exchanged type changes |
| `intro` (optional) | the list of services | endpoints are added/removed/retitled |

* Cache key = SHA-256(prompt version, unit kind, normalised payload). It does **not** include file paths or symbol ids,
  so moving/renaming files without changing content is free. Set `analysis.cache_key_includes_model: true` if you
  want a model switch to re-analyse everything.
* DTO units shared by many endpoints are described once; several small DTOs are batched in one call
  (`analysis.type_batch_tokens`); a batch that produces invalid JSON is split in half and retried.
* Budgets: `llm.max_calls_per_run`, `llm.max_input_tokens_per_run`. `analyze --dry-run` prints the plan first.
* `apidocgen graph impact path/to/File.java` tells you which endpoints a file change would touch.
* `render` never calls the LLM. If a unit changed and was not re-analysed yet, the previous analysis is used and the
  endpoint is marked `stale` (`analysis.stale_fallback`).
* `cache prune` removes analyses of code versions that no longer exist; `cache clear --unit <id>` forces one unit.

## 4. Configuration (`apidocgen.yaml`)

`apidocgen init` writes a fully commented template. Key sections:

```yaml
project:
  paths: [./src/main/java, ../shared-dtos/src/main/java]   # folders or files, absolute or relative
  include: ["**/*.java"]
  exclude: ["**/test/**", "**/target/**"]
  db: .apidocgen/graph.db            # graph + LLM cache + archive live next to it
  endpoints_file: endpoints.yaml     # optional manual endpoints (see §6)

detectors:
  spring: true
  jaxrs: true
  servlet: true
  micronaut: false
  custom:                            # in-house frameworks (see §5)
    - name: dispatcher
      class: { annotation: ServiceHandler }
      method: { name_regex: "^execute$" }
      http_method: { class_annotation_arg: method, default: POST }
      path: { class_annotation_arg: path }
      request: { param_index: 0 }
      response: { return_type: true }

analysis:
  call_depth: 1                      # how far to follow calls when slicing an endpoint
  max_slice_tokens: 6000
  type_batch_tokens: 9000
  error_catalog_regex: "(?i).*(ErrorCode|ResultCode|RsCode).*"   # enums/classes holding code+message pairs
  cache_key_includes_model: false
  stale_fallback: true

llm:
  provider: anthropic                # anthropic | openai | ollama | mock
  model: claude-sonnet-5
  api_key_env: ANTHROPIC_API_KEY
  base_url: null                     # openai-compatible: https://api.openai.com/v1, http://localhost:1234/v1 ...
  temperature: 0.2                   # null for models that reject it
  prompt_cache: true
  max_calls_per_run: null
  style_notes: ""                    # extra Persian terminology / style rules for the model

doc:
  title: "سرویس ثبت سند حسابداری"
  system_name: "سامانه بانکداری باز"
  organization: "داتین"
  classification: "عمومی"
  author: "نام تهیه‌کننده"
  date: auto                         # Jalali date of the render, or "1403/10/22"
  version: "1.17"
  logo: ./logo.png                   # embedded into the HTML
  font_google: Vazirmatn             # or font_file: ./Vazirmatn.ttf (embedded, offline)
  base_url: "http://[IP]:[PORT]"
  approvals: [...]                   # تاییدیه سند rows
  changelog: [...]                   # جدول تغییرات rows; changelog_auto appends added/changed/removed services
  common_headers: [...]              # ApiKey, Signature ... shown for every service
  common_error_codes: [...]          # 804, 805 ... appended to every error table
  intro_file: intro.md               # or intro_llm: true
  call_steps_file: null              # custom "مراحل فراخوانی سرویس" template ({method} {path} {body})
  include: []                        # endpoint id patterns, e.g. ["POST /API/*"]
  output: docs/api-document.html

overrides:                           # human corrections that survive every regeneration
  "POST /API/IssueDocument":
    title: "سرویس ثبت سند حسابداری"
    description: "..."
    notes: ["..."]                   # replaces LLM notes; extra_notes appends
    fields: { TransactionId: "شناسه یکتا تراکنش" }
    error_codes: [{ code: 1038, title: "اطلاعات ورودی اشتباه است" }]
    samples: { success: "{...}", failures: ["{...}"] }
    hide_fields: [InternalFlag]
```

Endpoint ids are `"<METHOD> <path>"` (see `apidocgen endpoints`).

## 5. Custom (in-house) frameworks

Anything that is *not* Spring/JAX-RS can be described with a rule instead of code:

```yaml
detectors:
  custom:
    - name: legacy
      class:  { extends: BaseHandler, name_regex: ".*Handler$" }   # annotation / extends / implements / name_regex
      method: { annotation: Handles }                              # annotation / name_regex / modifiers
      http_method: { annotation_arg: method, default: POST }       # read from the method or class annotation
      path: { annotation_arg: value, template: "/API/{class_name}", strip_suffix: Handler }
      request: { param_index: 0 }          # or param_annotation: Body, or none: true
      response: { base_type_arg: 1 }       # generic argument of BaseHandler<REQ, RES>, or return_type: true
      headers: [{ name: ApiKey, required: true, description: "..." }]
```

## 6. Manual endpoints & LLM-assisted discovery

For reflection/registry based routing the handler methods carry no markers. Either list them by hand:

```yaml
# endpoints.yaml
endpoints:
  - handler: com.bank.esb.legacy.ReverseHandler#execute   # class#method (parameter list optional)
    method: POST
    path: /Api/Reverse
    body: com.bank.esb.dto.ReverseRequest                 # optional
    response: com.bank.esb.dto.BaseResponse               # optional
```

or let the model propose them from **class skeletons only** (cheap, cached, written to that file for review):

```bash
apidocgen discover --class-regex ".*(Handler|Processor)$"
# review endpoints.yaml, set project.endpoints_file: endpoints.yaml, then: apidocgen scan
```

## 7. What is extracted without any LLM

* wire names (Jackson `@JsonProperty`, `@JsonNaming(UpperCamelCase/SnakeCase…)`, Gson `@SerializedName`),
  ignored fields (`@JsonIgnore`, `transient`), inherited fields (base class first, like serialisers),
  generic envelopes (`ApiResponse<T>` → `T` substituted), records, nested types, enums (constant + Persian title
  from the constructor argument / comment)
* required flags (`@NotNull/@NotBlank/@NotEmpty`, `@RequestParam(required)`, `@Schema(required…)`, primitives, path variables)
* descriptions from Javadoc, `//` comments, `@Schema/@ApiModelProperty/@Parameter` annotations
* error codes: constants of the error catalog (`ErrorCode.INVALID_INPUT` → `1038 – اطلاعات ورودی اشتباه است`) reached
  through the call graph from the handler (`controller → service → validate()` …)
* JSON samples (success / validation failure / business failure) from the response envelope
* headers, query, path, form, cookie parameters; Spring `RouterFunction` routes; servlet `getParameter/getHeader`

The LLM then adds: Persian service titles/descriptions, field descriptions and examples where the code has none,
enum labels, notes (conditional fields, balance rules…), and a Persian title for each error code.

### Business descriptions

`scan` extracts the API structure. `render` uses code comments and cached analyses; neither command invokes an LLM.
`mock` produces test placeholders, and `run --no-llm` skips analysis. To generate actual Persian business explanations,
configure a real `llm.provider` and `llm.model` with the required credentials, then run `apidocgen analyze` followed by
`apidocgen render` (or `apidocgen run`). In the UI use **Analyze**, with **dry-run** unchecked, then **Render**.

Set `project.description` to describe the application domain and `llm.style_notes` for terminology. The analyzer explains
service goals, preconditions and outcomes and all request/response fields, including nested fields, using the visible
implementation and validation rules. `analysis.call_depth: 2` includes validation methods called by services; increase
`analysis.max_slice_tokens` if necessary. Unsupported business rules must remain unspecified. Changing the application
context invalidates cached analyses; if `analysis.stale_fallback` is enabled, old text can remain visible as stale until
analysis finishes. Set it to `false` to show only current analyses. Explicit `overrides.fields` corrections still win.

## 8. Web UI

```bash
apidocgen ui                                  # one application (the config in the current folder)
apidocgen ui --workspace workspace.yaml       # several applications
```

```yaml
# workspace.yaml
applications:
  - { name: ESB Core,     config: ../esb-core/apidocgen.yaml }
  - { name: Card Service, config: ../card/apidocgen.yaml }
```

The UI (green `#2C673E` / orange `#E28B3C`, light theme with a dark-mode toggle) shows for each application the
endpoint tree (by path), endpoint details (tables, error codes, samples, analysis status, link into the latest
document), the archive of every generated document version (open/download/delete, what changed between versions),
status (graph, cache hits, token usage) and lets you run *Scan / Analyze (optionally dry-run) / Render / Run all*
with a live job log. It binds to `127.0.0.1` only.

## 9. The code graph

`.apidocgen/graph.db` (SQLite) holds files with content hashes, symbols (types, methods, fields, constants), edges
(`extends`, `implements`, `field_type`, `param_type`, `returns`, `throws`, `calls` — including interface → implementation,
`creates`, `uses_const`, `mentions`, `annotated_with`), detected endpoints, the analysis cache, LLM call log and
document snapshots.

```bash
apidocgen graph stats
apidocgen graph deps com.bank.esb.error.ErrorCode#INVALID_INPUT
apidocgen graph impact src/main/java/com/bank/esb/dto/MetaData.java
apidocgen graph export --format dot --out graph.dot      # or --format json
```

## 10. Notes & limitations

* Java only (Lombok-generated accessors are fine because documentation is field based; Kotlin/Groovy are not parsed).
* Values are read from source: an annotation argument referencing a constant from *another* file is kept as-is
  (`BASE + "/x"` inside the same class is resolved).
* `boolean isX` fields are documented by field name (`isX`); Jackson would expose `x` unless annotated — add
  `@JsonProperty` or an `overrides.fields` entry if that matters.
* Print layout is tuned for Chromium (Chrome/Edge). Google Fonts need internet when viewing; use `doc.font_file` to embed
  a font for offline/print use.
* The LLM never sees whole files: only the slices described in §3, truncated to the configured budgets.

## 11. Tests

```bash
pip install pytest && pytest -q            # or, without pytest:  python tests/run_tests.py
```

## 12. Layout

```
apidocgen/
  javaparse/    tokenizer + declaration-level Java parser (pure Python)
  graph/        SQLite store, in-memory index, graph builder (type/call resolution)
  detectors/    Spring / JAX-RS / Micronaut profiles, servlets, functional routes, custom rules, manual file
  docmodel/     deterministic document model: fields, naming, samples, error catalog
  analysis/     units + hashing, prompts, analyzer (cache, batching, budgets), discovery
  llm/          Anthropic / OpenAI-compatible / Ollama / mock clients (stdlib HTTP)
  render/       Jinja2 template → RTL HTML
  ui/           local web UI (stdlib http.server + single-page app)
  archive.py    document version archive
  pipeline.py   scan / analyze / render orchestration
  cli.py        command line
examples/sample-esb   a small ESB-like project covering all detector styles
```

## Local OpenAI setup

The default application is configured for `gpt-5.6-terra`. Run the following in your own Terminal:

```bash
cd /Users/manakhoshbatn/Downloads/apidocgen
.venv/bin/python setup_openai.py
```

Paste the key at the hidden prompt. The helper stores it in `.apidocgen/openai-key` with owner-only permissions;
this path is ignored by Git. The key is not printed and does not belong in chat, screenshots, or the main YAML file.
An `OPENAI_API_KEY` environment variable takes precedence over this file. After saving the key, use Analyze then
Render in the UI, or run `.venv/bin/python -m apidocgen run` to generate business descriptions.
